{"ex-209-1":{"number":"1","title":"Angular speed and linear speed","question":"<p>Amy and Sophie are respectively 3 m and 2 m away from the centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of a merry-go-round. They complete half a revolution in 10 s at a constant angular speed.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/25f82a25ce41fe2e2c626dc9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:444.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Find their angular speed in rad s<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(b) Find their angular displacement in 8 s. Express your answer in radians and degrees.</p><p>(c) Find their linear speeds.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Angular speed ω = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.314 rad s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(b) θ = ω<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 8 = 2.51 rad</p><p>Also,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>θ = 144°</p><p>(c) Amy’s linear speed = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω = 3 × 0.314 = 0.942 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Sophie’s linear speed = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω = 2 × 0.314 = 0.628 m s<sup>−1</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>θ for half a revolution = π</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 7","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":2},"id":"ex-209-1","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-2":{"number":"2","title":"Centripetal acceleration and radius","question":"<p>A sports car races at a constant speed of 40 m s<sup>−1</sup> on a track as shown. The track has two circular turns of different radii.</p><p>(a) Find the centripetal acceleration of the car at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) At which point, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, does the car have a greater acceleration? Explain.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8996ff5555ce21f927a35bea.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:149.5px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Centripetal acceleration = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 10.7 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>(b) The turn at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a smaller radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> , the car has a greater</p><p>acceleration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> for the same linear speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>Simon argues that the car has a smaller acceleration at Y according to a = rω², since the radius at Y is smaller. Explain why his argument is wrong.</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>The angular speed is not constant. The linear speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is fixed, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>ω</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> increases at the smaller radius. Substituting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Therefore the smaller radius at Y produces a greater acceleration.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":20},"id":"ex-209-2","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-3":{"number":"3","title":"Maximum speed for dry and wet roads","question":"<p>A car of 800 kg turns a corner of radius 20 m. The maximum sideways friction between the car and the road is 5500 N when the road is dry.</p><p>(a) Find the maximum speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the car without skidding.</p><p>(b) Why does the chance of skidding increase on rainy days?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = 11.7 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(b) The maximum friction between the car and the road decreases on rainy days. It is more likely that the friction is not large enough to provide the centripetal force required.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The car’s speed attains v<sub>max</sub> when F is maximum.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 20","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":34},"id":"ex-209-3","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-4":{"number":"4","title":"The turning of an aeroplane","question":"<p>An aeroplane of mass 4 × 10<sup>6</sup> kg makes a turn horizontally at a constant speed. The lifting force acting on it makes an angle θ to the  vertical.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/6f35592745fc1f9231ce4951.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:273.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the lifting force if θ = 5°.</p><p>(b) Find the centripetal force required to make the turn.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 3.94 × 10<sup>7</sup> N</p><p>(b) Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8427329343e8162912905f8f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:130.3px\"/></p><p>Centripetal force</p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin 5° = (3.94 × 10<sup>7</sup>) × sin 5° = 3.43 × 10<sup>6</sup> N</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The lifting force provides the centripetal force.</p>","think":"<p>Now, the mass of the aeroplane is reduced. If it follows the same path to make a turn with the same speed, how should θ be changed?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>The angle remains unchanged at 5°. From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The mass cancels. The required lift decreases in proportion to the mass, but its direction remains the same.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 23, 24","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":46},"id":"ex-209-4","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-5":{"number":"5","title":"Ideal banking angle","question":"<p>A corner of a highway has a radius of curvature of 580 m. The mean speed of the cars passing along the highway is 20 m s<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Find the ideal banking angle of this corner.</p><p>(b) A car of mass 1500 kg turns the corner at 20 m s<sup>−1</sup>. Find the normal force acting on it by the highway if the corner is banked at the ideal banking angle.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ..................... (1)</p><p>Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ...................... (2)</p><p>Divide (1) by (2):</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/cc47afd7dce3e5a4a0481aff.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:123.2px\"/></p><p>tan θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>θ = 4.02°</p><p>The ideal banking angle is 4.02°.</p><p>(b) From (2):</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 14 800 N</p><p>The normal force acting on it by the highway is 14 800 N.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 26","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":69},"id":"ex-209-5","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-6":{"number":"6","title":"Verifying T = mω2L with the centripetal force apparatus","question":"<p>Refer to the set-up in Experiment 9a. The mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the rubber bung is 20 g. The total mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the screw nuts is 1 kg. The length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the string is at an angle θ to the vertical.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/49db429645e80f0cc8141909.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:332.9px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the tension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in the string.</p><p>(b) How would <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and θ change if the experiment is repeated with a smaller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>? Explain briefly.</p><p>(c) Show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Hence, determine how ω would change if the</p><p>experiment is repeated with a smaller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) As the screw nuts remain at rest, the net force acting on them is zero.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>= 1 × 9.81</p><p>= 9.81 N</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e435a250b3a3364d1c799c34.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:60.7px\"/></p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> would remain unchanged as it is still equal to the weight of the screw nuts.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ is still equal to the weight of the rubber bung. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> remains unchanged, θ also remains unchanged.</p><p>(c) Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/551ca8afa7516085b789b1b3.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:138.7px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ) × ω<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>According to the equation, ω would become larger if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> becomes smaller.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>It is important to identify the quantities which have fixed values in a certain situation.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":90},"id":"ex-209-6","chapter":"209"},"ex-209-7":{"number":"7","title":"Toy plane tied to the ceiling","question":"<p>A toy plane of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is whirled in a horizontal circular path of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at a constant angular speed ω. The tension in the string is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and the string makes an angle θ with the vertical.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/082ce3ea5b4784281081fa56.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:340.8px\"/></p><p>(a) Show that cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) How will θ change if the toy plane is whirled more quickly?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ) × ω<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ………………(1)</p><p>Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ………………(2)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/870aa15dd2ce401f3d5aa267.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:138.7px\"/></p><p>Substitute (1) into (2):</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>ω<sup>2</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>(b) θ will increase.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>According to cos θ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, for constant L, ω ↑ ⇒ cos θ ↓ ⇒ θ ↑</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 209  |  Source PDF p. 34","source":{"name":"209 Examples.docx","sha256":"bb2642e3da05d13a18a117031046c6d702e8bf55437e156420e541afa3c73a61","block":115},"id":"ex-209-7","chapter":"209"}}